ies in the Spanish-speaking world and a significant institution in the development of higher education in Europe. Its establishment coincided with the height of the Renaissance, a period marked by rediscovery of classical knowledge and the flourishing of arts and sciences. The university played a critical role in shaping educational standards and academic discourse, contributing to the intellectual and cultural life of Southern Europe, particularly in Galicia.

